commentBody:
@NYC Traveler
Pretty good guess, Trav. 7.30 AM on Friday and we're at 368. 
Congrats and thanks to the multiple voices of the commentariat.

@Jake G

Apt metaphor – though perhaps potato chips are a little sharper, and crispier, than this puzzle... I'd say more like really good mashed potatoes: smooth, easily digestible, but not distasteful.

I have enjoyed Jill Singer's previous puzzles a lot. This one felt spoon-fed in the cluing department! Like the authors were being extra, extra careful to have solvers "get" it, especially around the themers. I'd be very curious what percentage of clues were original to the constructor!

@SP

I was able to get enough clues for my not knowing the second revealer to not really matter - I had enough crosses to infer the title from the theme (the theme revealed the revealer, not the other way round). Especially the NW corner was easy to deal with for me. I've seen enough Thursdays at the NYT for the rather unusual theme to not confuse me - in fact, I enjoyed the novelty of it. Now I understand what a skilled constructor you really are.

Just please, PLEASE, don't oversature your grids with trivia (beyond what's really necessary, like for today's theme to work). When there is less of it, the puzzles are much more enjoyable (to me, anyway, but nothing else matters - to me :P). There are few worse feelings in the cruciverbial realm thah failing a solve because you don't know a random name.

@HeathieJ
People are always so snooty about 'symptom relief.' Well, after years of close observation, I can tell you that people with symptoms are mighty glad to get relief and not at all disdainful. /Of course/ they'd prefer a cure but, until one is found for whatever ails you, symptom relief can let you live more of your life; no small gift.
Get well soon, Ms Heathie.

@HeathieJ

I think you’ve found the trick: when life hands you lemons… find the people who got the vodka and the triple sec, and throw a fabulous party. 

I’m so glad that you’ve managed to keep your sense of humor through all of that. When I think of all the times you’ve radiated positivity and cheer here, even though you may have felt less than optimal, I’m also more than a little impressed. Cheers to you and your hip-hips, for always being ready to har-rumba!

@Andree
Not!
Quick AND fun.

@Bryan

Yes. I had no idea what the things in DRUM KIT'S clue were - the clue was basically useless for me. As for old McDonald and his farm: it came up once in my English lessons almost 40 years ago - why would I remember it?

My solving experience as a non-native English speaker living in Poland is totally unlike yours, I bet.

@Andrzej

For the record no I didn’t get AEIOU without most of the crosses, although I may have if it had been one word like “facetious”

Curious l, there was a famous Polish baseball player named Carl Yaszstremski whose name was always pronounced with a hard Y. Is this not standard Polish, or would his name there have been pronounced as EE-as…?

@Jim,

I’m guessing the reply was meant for 
@Mike R

@lucky13

Hey, and another no lookups, gold star for today's bonus puzzle, "The Answer, My Friend" and, of course, more music!

