@Sydney
Think the written phrase "gathering clouds," which contains all the vowels, in order: AEIOU. The quotation marks would be there on a Monday or Tuesday, but sneakily left off later in the week.
(It took me several crosses to see what was going on.)

@Hitch

Summary from Google Gemini:

"The Southern Paiute and the Ute people speak dialects of the Colorado River Numic language, which are so closely linked that they are mutually intelligible. Historically, the primary distinction between the groups was cultural rather than linguistic—those who adopted horses became known as the Ute, while those who remained on foot were referred to as Southern Paiute."

@Joe

Sidney LUMET made many very well-known films, including "12 Angry Men" which appeared in a puzzle recently. Pick one.

<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidney_Lumet_filmography" target="_blank">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidney_Lumet_filmography</a>

@Jordan

Marked rebuses are the pits. Marking one negates the challenge of having the rebus in the first place. Why have an interesting feature and spoil it by gimme circles?

It took me a year to get used to rebuses but now they are one of my favorite aspects of crosswords.

@Jordan
FYI, it seems the people behind the app may be getting more lenient.  I did not have the proper rebus in for the first theme answer (I had "F" instead of "FALL") and still finished the puzzle.  

Details:  I entered PLEDGEOF and then when I got the the NE corner I got ELIGIANCE.  I figured I'd come back to it to figure out where and what the rebus was (which I personally think is part of the fun), but when I finished the rest, I got the victory jingle.  Having "F" in the rebus square was enough.

@Jordan,

As much as I love rebus puzzles, I agree with the solution that you’ve come up with. It lets solvers who want to know that a dreaded rebus is present, but the hint is completely optional and comes at the cost of breaking one’s streak (rightfully so). 

Seems like a win-win to me. I like it.

@Jordan
That horse is way out of the barn, with its crusader astride.
Bye, noble steed.

@Joe,

I will await your replies for the people who post “Nice [Wednesday/Tuesday/Monday] puzzle!” on Thursday and Fridays. :)

I bet I won’t see anything.

@Barry Ancona

What exactly is your goal here? 
Sorry you have nothing better to do.

@Bill
Thanks for the omnium-gatherum, and in particular for referring to the TOGA's sinuses. It reminded me of the relation to 'sinuous' and even further back to the sine wave.
Ah, language!

@Helen Wright

FOCI are central points.  LOCI are just points.

I think the author you've read was Gay TALESE.  An unfortunate nickname for Gaetano, assuming you're heterosexual, but he's 94 years old.

@Steve L
Ah, maybe. Odd name to my Brit ears.
FOCI/loci. I know. Brain not fully engaged this morning!

@Bill

Is it possible that you have a problem with carpenter bees?  The woodpeckers go after the larva and do quite a bit of damage. 

We had bees in the trim on one side of our house and tried to get rid of them for three years.  Then the woodpeckers came and made swiss cheese out of the trim.  We gave in and replaced it with a pvc product.  Once painted, it looks just like the rest of the house.

@Heg

The "excuse" is simple:  the name, which is a transliteration from ancient Greek, can be spelled either way, and how it's spelled in the puzzle depends on what is needed for the cross.  Since "kem" isn't a thing, "I" it is today.

It's merely coincidental that the two puzzles in which the name was spelled two different ways came one after the other.

@I'm Outa

Crossword puzzles are not for everyone.  But I've done crosswords on a variety of platforms, and I can't see how NYT crosswords are substantially different from other ones.

Sorry you don't like them; you don't have to.  I do, and I've been solving for almost 50 years.  If you don't, leaving might be your best option.

@I'm Outa

I like rebus puzzles as long as they're reasonable rebusses.  Just adds a second layer of puzzle on top of the puzzle.

When I don't like it is when they suddenly start adding numbers or punctuation, or have a single square rebus on a non-Thursday.  On Thursday though you know the rules enough to look for them.

@Matt

Did you even read the original post, and notice the poster’s handle?  And sign off?

As usual, in a rush to be a gadfly, you’re missing something vital.

@Luke
It might be annoying to some but is isn't lazy...it's more difficult for a designer to find rebuses of a specific theme fit both up and down.  Maybe you're lazy for not buckling down to figure them out...

There is a large group of people, silent and loud (like me) who think rebuses are fun.  Don't blame the designers or even the NYT.  Blame us.

@Charles Peterson
Good point.  Nice deduction.


<&:-#

@Chris TAT is short for tattoo, and is a good one to keep in your crossword quiver. It shows up frequently. 

As for GAIA/GAEA, I had the same thought.  It’s not quite a kealoa, but there are a handful like it (gray/gray) where I have to wait for the cross. 

(I might have to add PAIUTE to my list - I’ve always seen it spelled Payute. But as I type this, autocorrect wants to change that Y to an I, so maybe I’m just wrong. 😊)

@Darren, I'm not sure how old you are (I'm 66), but all four names you listed are quite well known to me, and IMO, are mostly deducible from the crosses, especially Ravel. 

Also, not sure why so many don't like 16A, but it's also pretty easy with crosses. 

This was a very straightforward Thursday for me; I finished nearly 10 minutes under my average.

@Darren

Off topic, but didn't you say you were training for a marathon?

If it's the Twin Cities, then it's this weekend.  Good luck!

@Barry Ancona

It's true, but LUMET is not reading, (although I have read the script for "12 Angry Men"), RAVEL is not reading, CELIA is not reading. ABBA is not reading, MOE is not reading, OINK is not reading. There are lots of ways to learn things!

I got them all right today! Yea!

